# sparse_blocks_normal_font_arial_9.xlsx -- FROZEN EXPECTATION.
# A reading of the openpyxl-authored fixture against plan 01 D2/D3/D4.
# Corrected ONLY when that reading was wrong -- never edited to chase an FSA1 regression.
#
# The reading, in three steps:
#
# 1. `<cellStyle name="Normal" xfId="0"/>` -> cellStyleXfs[0] -> fontId 1 ->
#    `<font><name val="Arial"/><sz val="9"/></font>`, so the workbook's Normal font is Arial 9, as in
#    `normal_font_arial_9`. Each of the four `<c>` carries `s="1"` -> that same fontId, and nothing else.
#
# 2. OCCUPANCY is the four coordinates holding a value: A1, E5, A56, E60. No style in the sheet states
#    a fill or an edge, and those are the only two looks that show on a cell holding no value, so no
#    blank is content. The bounding box A1:E60 is 5x60 = 300 cells over 4 occupied, past the 256 waste
#    budget, and the widest gap is the 50 empty rows 6..55 rather than the three empty columns B..D --
#    so the cut is there: TWO blocks, A1:E5 and A56:E60, each 25 cells and under budget. 23 of each 25
#    are blank, which is the shape neither `normal_font_arial_9` (2x2, fully occupied) nor
#    `style_only_blank` (its blank carries a fill) holds.
#
# 3. A cell stating no style wears the workbook's Normal font, so all 25 cells of each block wear
#    Arial 9 -- including the blanks. Arial 9 is not the format's own default (Calibri 11), which is
#    what a writer restores for an undeclared cell, so both halves are DECLARED, uniform over the
#    block, as ONE `td` rule. dk1 is `windowText`/000000, so the text colour IS the default and earns
#    no declaration.
file: Gaps/A1:E5
|1				
|				
|				
|				
|				2
|@scope {
|  td { font-family: Arial; font-size: 9pt }
|}
file: Gaps/A56:E60
|3				
|				
|				
|				
|				4
|@scope {
|  td { font-family: Arial; font-size: 9pt }
|}
